summary:
- topic: SPHS Athletic Complex Discussion
  text: The board reviewed follow-up information on the SPHS Athletic Complex, including
    storage building costs, track replacement feasibility, coach training for synthetic
    turf, and PFAS testing protocols. Superintendent Atkinson Duina provided updated
    cost estimates for natural grass ($7.29M) vs. synthetic turf ($8.62M).
- topic: Environmental & Financial Concerns for Turf
  text: Board members and public commenters raised concerns about PFAS, microplastics,
    and the project's alignment with the city's 'One Climate Future' plan. Questions
    were posed regarding the financial logic of a long bond for a shorter-lifespan
    product and the need for objective information on organic grass alternatives.
- topic: Student Perspective on Field Condition
  text: A student representative highlighted the unsafe and unusable condition of
    the current high school and Wainwright fields, arguing that artificial turf is
    safer and allows for more athletic use. Other public comments questioned the 'all
    or nothing' approach to the referendum and the environmental impact of artificial
    turf.
- topic: 'State of the Schools: Demographics & Immigration'
  text: Superintendent Atkinson Duina presented data showing a significant increase
    in students of color (37%) and multilingual learners (16%) since 2019. He outlined
    the district's response to federal immigration policy changes, emphasizing schools
    as places of learning and the district's commitment to diversity, equity, and
    inclusion (DEI).
- topic: Student Attendance & Behavior Improvements
  text: The presentation revealed a decrease in chronic absenteeism to 18% and a dramatic
    reduction in out-of-school suspensions, attributed to restorative practices and
    improved school climate. The board praised these successes, noting the positive
    impact of Positive Behavioral Interventions and Supports (PBIS).
- topic: Academic Performance & Post-Secondary Trends
  text: NWEA data indicated consistent percentages of students needing support in
    reading and math, prompting continued focus on literacy. Post-secondary plans
    showed a significant increase in students attending two-year colleges, largely
    due to free tuition, reflecting a positive trend in college-bound students.
- topic: Student & Family Perceptions of School Climate
  text: Survey results showed high student satisfaction (74%) with diversity and inclusion,
    and favorable family ratings for elementary school safety and interpersonal relationships.
    Overall, positive trends were observed in school climate and students' sense of
    belonging, with slight increases across most categories.
- topic: Student Belonging & Segregation Concerns
  text: A student representative raised concerns about the segregation of special
    education and multilingual students at the high school, suggesting it impacts
    their sense of belonging. Board members discussed the need for greater inclusion
    and staff connection to foster a more unified school community.
